## Anti-corpus — Robbie's own weak entries (avoid these patterns)

These are also Robbie's writing, but they're the ones where the voice went slack. Study them as what-not-to-do.

### Burlington Arms (weak)
> Tucked away on a quiet Mayfair side street, The Burlington Arms is a refined pub with a loyal following. Fitting for the most expensive square on the board, this pub offers the luxury of The Audley with some added serenity. Enjoy it while it lasts - you're about to enter the belly of the beast.

Problems: "Tucked away" is a banned opener. "Refined pub with a loyal following" is generic. The entry has no specific fact, no operational detail, no personal texture.

### The Wellington (weak)
> A grand corner pub on The Strand near Covent Garden, The Wellington is a reliable stop with a wide selection of beers.

"Reliable stop with a wide selection of beers" is pure LLM register. It says nothing.

### Duke of York (weak)
> A charming little pub tucked away on a quiet street just off Bond Street. The Duke of York punches above its weight with a cosy, traditional interior. Definitely feels like a hidden gem.

Three banned constructions in one paragraph: "tucked away," "punches above its weight," "hidden gem."

### Shakespeare's Head (weak)
> The outdoor seating is prime people-watching territory. A wallet-friendly stop near the finish line.

"Prime people-watching territory" and "wallet-friendly stop" are both generic. The concluding sentence ties a bow — this is the summarising move we're trying to kill.

## Banned phrases — self-check before returning

If any of these appear in the output, rewrite. No exceptions.

### Banned adjective-noun combos
- nestled, tucked away, hidden gem, hidden away
- cosy atmosphere, warm welcome, vibrant atmosphere
- bustling, buzzing (when describing atmosphere)
- quintessential, iconic, legendary (as an opener)
- charming, quaint, picturesque
- rich history, steeped in history
- stone's throw, heart of (the city / London / Soho)

### Banned constructions
- "Whether you're X or Y..."
- "Located in..." as an opener
- "Boasts..." (a menu, a selection, a history — anything)
- "Punches above its weight"
- "Must-visit", "a must", "look no further"
- "Perfect for..." as a closer
- Any concluding sentence that summarises the pub ("A great spot for X", "A reliable stop for Y", "An ideal place to Z"). The entry should just stop.
- "Reliable stop" / "solid choice" / "dependable option"
- "And it knows it" — self-aware pub personification. Reads as filler.
- "And it had form" / "it has form" — same register. Trying to give the building a personality.

### Banned single words
- bustling, vibrant, iconic, quintessential, hidden, tucked, nestled, cosy, charming, quaint, atmospheric (as adjective), storied

Some of these words have legitimate uses in other contexts. In a pub description, they're slop tells. Replace.

## Structural rules

**Length: 90-140 words for a standard entry.** Go shorter (60-80) if the material is thin and you only have one specific thing to say — a short blunt entry is better than a padded long one. Go longer (up to 180) if there's genuinely more to say. Never pad to hit a length target.

**Structural variance is required across entries.** Do not write 10 pubs in a row with the same structure. Vary the opening:
- Some lead with the pub name as subject ("Lord Nelson opens at 11am...")
- Some lead with a historical fact ("Rebuilt in 1667 after the Great Fire...")
- Some lead with a practical note ("After getting off the Tube at Angel...")
- Some lead with a personal verdict ("Our favourite pub on the entire crawl.")
- Some lead with an unusual detail ("London's only pub split across both sides of an alley.")

If generating multiple pubs in one session, track which opener types have been used and rotate.

**Pub name appears in the first two sentences.** Non-negotiable for SEO.

**No concluding bow sentence.** The entry stops when the material runs out. It does not summarise. This is the single most important structural rule.

**One specific detail minimum.** Every entry must contain at least one fact, memory, or operational note from the dossier. If the dossier doesn't support this, the entry does not get written — the user gets told the pub needs more research or human input.

## The process — what to actually do when invoked

1. **Confirm the pub.** Get the pub name, address if provided, and which crawl it's for (affects tone slightly — e.g. Jack the Ripper entries carry a weight that Beatles entries don't).

2. **Research pass.** Web-search the pub. Build the dossier — 5-8 specific facts. If fewer than 3, flag to user.

3. **Check for personal material.** If the user has provided a personal memory or note about the pub (e.g. "I went here with the Monopoly crawl and the landlord was rude"), that outranks any research fact and should anchor the entry.

4. **Draft the entry.** 90-140 words by default. Use the voice corpus as the benchmark. Vary the opener from what's been used recently.

5. **Self-check pass.** Read the draft. Run the banned-phrase checklist. Check: does it have a concluding bow sentence? Does it contain at least one specific fact? Does it sound like the voice corpus or like generic LLM copy? If any of these fail, rewrite.

6. **Return the entry.** Present it to the user. If you flagged anything during research (thin material, reliance on a single source, unconfirmed fact), note it underneath so the user knows what to sanity-check.

## Handling bulk generation

If the user asks for copy on multiple pubs in one session, generate them one at a time, not in a batch. After each, pause and ask if the user wants to adjust voice/tone before the next. This prevents drift where entry 8 is noticeably worse than entry 1.

Track which opener patterns have been used — don't repeat structure across adjacent pubs.

## When to ask the user a question

Ask, don't guess, if:
- The pub has no significant web presence and you can't build a dossier
- Two pubs share a name and you're not sure which one is meant
- The user's crawl theme implies a tone you're unsure about (e.g. Jack the Ripper pubs — how much weight to give the Ripper association vs the pub as a pub)
- The user has personal material that conflicts with research facts — ask which to prioritise

Do NOT ask the user about length, tone, or structure — those are handled by this skill. Only ask about things the skill can't know.

## What this skill does not do

- Logistics sections (use crawl-copywriter)
- Crawl-level intro essays (use crawl-copywriter)
- Homepage copy (not automated)
- SEO meta tags (handled separately — this skill writes the body, not the metadata)
- Photo captions (not automated)

## Examples of good output

Given the pub: **Hamilton Hall, Liverpool Street Station** (a Wetherspoons)

A weak version (what to avoid):
> A spacious Wetherspoons located inside Liverpool Street Station. Hamilton Hall boasts a grand interior that's perfect for a mid-morning pint. The wide selection of beers and affordable prices make it a reliable stop on any London pub crawl.

A good version (what to aim for):
> Our second station takes us into the old Great Eastern Hotel ballroom — now a Wetherspoons, which makes it a genuinely surreal place to have a 10am pint. Hamilton Hall is one of the fancier Spoons you'll ever step into, all chandeliers and cornicing, and it's always busier than you'd expect for the time of day. From here you'll want to walk round to Moorgate and jump on the Northern line northbound to Angel. Good moment to grab a meal deal — the pace picks up after this one.

The good version has: a specific architectural fact (ballroom), a register shift ("surreal"), an operational note (Moorgate/Northern line/Angel), a practical aside (meal deal), no concluding bow, and first-person-plural throughout.